{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Young1961-Riblet-3layer-n227",
  "Source": "L. Young, \"Synthesis of multiple antireflection films over a prescribed frequency band,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 51, 967-974 (1961). doi:10.1364/JOSA.51.000967 - Text pp. 970-972 (Eqs. 12-26) and Fig. 3, p. 972: n1 = 1.13 / 1179 A, n2 = 1.50 / 888 A, n3 = 2.02 / 659 A, substrate n = 2.27",
  "Notes": "Worked example from network synthesis: 3-layer Tschebyscheff (equiripple) antireflection film from air to n = 2.27, amplitude reflection <= 0.025 for all wavelengths 4080-7672 A.\nSpecification: Amplitude reflection coefficient |r| <= 0.025 (R <= 6.25e-4) for all free-space wavelengths 4080-7672 A; equiripple (Tschebyscheff) response, quarter-wave layers at band centre.\nReported: Eq. (12): W = 0.611, mu0 = 0.464, (n-1)^2/(4n T3^2(1/mu0)) = 1.578e-4 = maximum |r|^2/(1-|r|^2) in band, i.e. max ripple R ~ 1.58e-4 (|r| ~ 0.0126), well under the 0.025 spec; Table I gives max |r| = 0.01 for k = 3, W = 0.60, n = 2.00-2.50 (p. 972 text: 'its maximum reflection coefficient is only 0.01'). Eq. (19): r = (1-n)/(1+n) at s = 0 (infinite wavelength), i.e. R = 0.1504 for the bare n = 2.27 surface; this is also the maximum R reached outside the passband (at wavelengths where the layers are half waves, e.g. 266.4 nm; p. 974). Physical thicknesses 1179, 888, 659 A with mean quarter wavelength l0 = 1332 A (Eq. 27). Derived (not printed): Tschebyscheff zeros of R at cos(theta) = 0 and +/-0.866 mu0, i.e. lambda ~ 532.8, ~722 and ~422 nm; ripple maxima R ~ 1.5e-4 at 408, ~464, ~626 and 767.2 nm.\nNotes: Fig. 3 draws the outermost, lowest-index layer (n1 = 1.13) against the incident medium of index 1 and n3 next to the n = 2.27 medium; listed in that order. Eq. (25) prints n3 = 2.20, but Fig. 3 shows n3 = 2.02 and the printed thickness 659 A = 1332/2.02 (and Eq. 30 gives 2.27/1.13 = 2.01), so 2.20 is a misprint and 2.02 is used. Thicknesses are the printed values (A -> nm); each is l0/n_i with l0 = lambda1*lambda2/(2(lambda1+lambda2)) = 133.2 nm, so every layer is a quarter wave at reference_nm = 4*l0 = 532.8 nm (QWOT = 1 within rounding). All media lossless with constant index; normal incidence; air (n = 1) incident medium, n = 2.27 semi-infinite exit medium (no back surface). The paper computes mu0 = 0.464, while sin(0.611*45 deg) = 0.4617 (exact band edges then fall at 408.0 and 767.2 nm).\nLayers from Text pp. 970-972 (Eqs. 12-26) and Fig. 3, p. 972: n1 = 1.13 / 1179 A, n2 = 1.50 / 888 A, n3 = 2.02 / 659 A, substrate n = 2.27; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours max |r| 0.0182 over 408-767 nm; R 0.1508 at 266.4 nm; paper max |r| 0.01 against the 0.025 asked for (p. 972); R 0.1504 where the layers are half waves. The indices are printed to three figures, which is enough to move a ripple of 0.01.",
